## Current macOS path

1. **The native event reaches `WKWebView` as an `NSEvent`.**  
   `WKWebView` forwards `keyDown:` into `WebViewImpl::keyDown`; the current implementation then runs the event through AppKit text interpretation before creating the cross-process event. The relevant entry points are [`WKWebViewMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/mac/WKWebViewMac.mm#L1366-L1371) and [`WebViewImpl::interpretKeyEvent`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L5920-L6007).

2. **`NativeWebKeyboardEvent` preserves the `NSEvent`.**  
   Its constructor calls `WebEventFactory::createWebKeyboardEvent(...)` and retains the native event in `m_nativeEvent`: [`NativeWebKeyboardEventMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/Shared/mac/NativeWebKeyboardEventMac.mm#L35-L43).

3. **The complete `NSEvent.characters` string becomes `WebKeyboardEvent.text`.**  
   There is no truncation in this conversion:

   ```objective-c++
   String textFromEvent(NSEvent *event, bool replacesSoftSpace)
   {
       if (replacesSoftSpace)
           return emptyString();
       if ([event type] == NSEventTypeFlagsChanged)
           return emptyString();
       return String([event characters]);
   }
   ```

   [`PlatformEventFactoryMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/platform/mac/PlatformEventFactoryMac.mm#L241-L248)

   That complete string is copied into the IPC event:

   ```objective-c++
   String text = WebCore::textFromEvent(event, replacesSoftSpace);
   String unmodifiedText = WebCore::unmodifiedTextFromEvent(event, replacesSoftSpace);
   ```

   [`WebEventFactory.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/Shared/mac/WebEventFactory.mm#L219-L230)

   Therefore, for an `NSEvent` whose `characters` is the two-UTF-16-code-unit string `@"ãn"`, `WebKeyboardEvent.text` is also `ãn`. WebKit does **not** generally reduce that field to its first UTF-16 unit.

4. **One native keydown produces one DOM `keydown`, and ordinarily one derived `keypress`.**  
   `EventHandler::internalKeyEvent` converts the combined native keydown into one `RawKeyDown`, constructs one `KeyboardEvent`, and dispatches it:

   ```cpp
   keyDownEvent.disambiguateKeyDownEvent(PlatformEvent::Type::RawKeyDown, ...);
   auto keydown = KeyboardEvent::create(keyDownEvent, &frame->windowProxy());
   ...
   element->dispatchEvent(keydown);
   ```

   [`EventHandler.cpp`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/page/EventHandler.cpp#L4295-L4304), [`EventHandler.cpp`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/page/EventHandler.cpp#L4350-L4357)

   If it is not handled as IME input, WebKit then derives **one** Char/keypress event from the same platform event:

   ```cpp
   PlatformKeyboardEvent keyPressEvent = initialKeyEvent;
   keyPressEvent.disambiguateKeyDownEvent(PlatformEvent::Type::Char, ...);
   ...
   auto keypress = KeyboardEvent::create(keyPressEvent, ...);
   element->dispatchEvent(keypress);
   ```

   [`EventHandler.cpp`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/page/EventHandler.cpp#L4376-L4413)

   It does **not** loop over UTF-16 units and generate a separate DOM key event for each character.

5. **The normal editing default action inserts the command’s complete text.**  
   macOS editing callbacks are represented as `KeypressCommand`s. For `insertText:` WebKit passes the entire `currentCommand.text` to `Editor::insertText`:

   ```cpp
   if (currentCommand.commandName == "insertText:"_s) {
       ...
       eventWasHandled |= editor->insertText(currentCommand.text, event);
   }
   ```

   [`WebPageMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/mac/WebPageMac.mm#L287-L318)

   `Editor::insertText` forwards the string without slicing:

   ```cpp
   bool Editor::insertText(const String& text, Event* triggeringEvent, ...)
   {
       return document().frame()->eventHandler().handleTextInputEvent(
           text, triggeringEvent, inputType);
   }
   ```

   [`Editor.cpp`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/editing/Editor.cpp) (`Editor::insertText`)

   `handleTextInputEvent` constructs one text-input event carrying the whole string and dispatches it to the focused target. Thus, if the AppKit command is `insertText:@"ãn"`, the editing layer is designed to insert the whole `ãn` string as one editing operation.

## Important distinction: text versus legacy key identity

The complete text is retained, but some legacy keyboard metadata is inherently single-character:

- `windowsKeyCodeForKeyEvent` examines only `[characters characterAtIndex:0]`: [`PlatformEventFactoryMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/platform/mac/PlatformEventFactoryMac.mm#L591-L616).
- `keyIdentifierForKeyEvent` explicitly requires `charactersIgnoringModifiers.length == 1`; otherwise it logs an unexpected character count and returns `"Unidentified"`:

  ```objective-c++
  if ([string length] != 1) {
      LOG(Events, "received an unexpected number of characters...");
      return "Unidentified"_s;
  }
  ```

  [`PlatformEventFactoryMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/platform/mac/PlatformEventFactoryMac.mm#L543-L548)

This is likely the source of older assumptions that a keyboard event is “one character.” It applies to key identity/keyCode compatibility fields, **not** to the text payload or insertion command. A multi-character event can therefore have:

- one DOM `keydown`;
- one DOM `keypress` at most;
- an `"Unidentified"` or first-character-derived legacy identity;
- but a full multi-character insertion payload.

## Role of `NSTextInputContext`

WebKit does not simply trust `NSEvent.characters` and immediately insert it. The macOS UI-process path invokes AppKit text interpretation, and records callbacks such as:

- `insertText:replacementRange:`
- `setMarkedText:...`
- `doCommandBySelector:`

`WebViewImpl::insertText` documents the distinction:

```objective-c++
// - If it's from normal key event processing (including key bindings), we save the action...
// - If it's from an input method, then we should insert the text now.
// - If it's sent outside of keyboard event processing ... execute it immediately.
```

[`WebViewImpl.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L6026-L6032), [`WebViewImpl.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L6034-L6079)

During key interpretation it queues the full `NSString` and replacement range:

```objective-c++
WebCore::KeypressCommand command(
    "insertText:"_s, text.get(), { }, { }, { },
    EditingRange { replacementRange }.toCharacterRange());
m_collectedKeypressCommands.first().append(command);
```

[`WebViewImpl.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L6063-L6079)

Consequently, the actual result **does depend on `NSTextInputContext`/AppKit interpretation**:

- If AppKit calls `insertText:@"ãn"`, WebKit queues and inserts all of `ãn`.
- If AppKit interprets the unusual synthesized event as composition, partial insertion, or multiple callbacks, WebKit follows those callbacks rather than blindly inserting `NSEvent.characters`.
- If the input method says it handled the event, WebKit follows its handled/composition route and may suppress the ordinary keypress route.

This is particularly relevant to a CGEvent containing multiple Unicode characters: that is not equivalent to calling the `NSTextInputClient` method `insertText:@"ãn"` directly.

## Current modeless-IME behavior

Current `WebViewImpl::interpretKeyEvent` contains explicit logic for Vietnamese Simple Telex and other modeless input methods:

```cpp
if (handled && hasOnlyInsertText && !editorState().hasComposition)
    handled = NO;
```

It routes a pure modeless `insertText:` through the ordinary keypress/Char phase so insertion has the correct underlying event and DOM ordering. The surrounding comments explicitly name Simple Telex and Korean Hangul: [`WebViewImpl.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L5935-L5980).

It also compares the total UTF-16 length of queued IME insertion commands with the native event’s `characters.length`:

```objective-c++
insertTextLengthFromInputMethod += command.text.length();
NSUInteger eventCharactersLength = [[capturedEvent characters] length];
inputMethodCommittedPartialInsertText =
    insertTextLengthFromInputMethod < eventCharactersLength;
```

[`WebViewImpl.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L5935-L5958)

That logic is affirmative evidence that current WebKit expects a single native event’s characters string to contain more text than one insertion command and can preserve a trailing suffix; it is not based on a one-code-unit invariant.

Replacement ranges from modeless IMEs are also preserved and applied before insertion: [`WebPageMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/mac/WebPageMac.mm#L306-L317).

## Synthetic `CGEvent` relevance

There is no special production branch in WebKit that recognizes “this keyboard event was made by `CGEventCreateKeyboardEvent`” or reads `CGEventKeyboardSetUnicodeString` directly. The relevant boundary is:

1. macOS converts/posts the `CGEvent`;
2. AppKit creates and dispatches an `NSEvent`;
3. WebKit reads `NSEvent.characters`, `keyCode`, modifiers, and runs it through the normal `NSTextInputContext` path.

Thus a tap-generated event is subject to all normal AppKit interpretation and key-layout consistency checks. A Unicode payload such as `ãn` paired with one physical virtual key code is not treated by WebKit as two independent keystrokes.

WebKit’s own automation implementation explicitly warns against conflating synthetic per-character key events with complex text input: its comments describe automation insertion as similar to `insertText:` and note that emitting keydown/up/keypress “for roughly each character” has no similarity to how keyboards input complex text; see [`WebAutomationSessionMac.mm`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/Automation/mac/WebAutomationSessionMac.mm).

## Historical assumptions versus current behavior

Recent history materially changed this area:

- **313212@main / `300105f…`** stopped forcing every IME `insertText:` result into the unhandled keypress route. Composition commits now remain handled, use keyCode 229, and execute their queued insertion despite no keypress. The commit explains that the earlier assumption caused Japanese commits to beep or trigger Google search.
- **313286@main / `4951276…`** fixed modeless Vietnamese/Korean input by:
  - preserving `replacementRange`;
  - staging selection changes expected by modeless IMEs;
  - routing pure modeless `insertText:` through the Char phase;
  - adding tests for `"vi"` replacing `"v"`.
- **315032@main / `98e37d…`** added a temporary macOS 27 workaround that disables “correct event ordering” for Apple Simple Telex and 2-Set Korean on sites requiring composition events: [`WebViewImpl::inputMethodUsesCorrectKeyEventOrder`](https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/mac/WebViewImpl.mm#L3793-L3807).

So an old conclusion such as “WebKit only accepts the first character from an `NSEvent`” is not valid for current insertion behavior. The accurate current statement is:

> WebKit preserves the full `NSEvent.characters` string, generates one DOM keyboard-event sequence for the one native event, and inserts the full text supplied by AppKit’s `insertText:` callback. It does not split the string into per-character DOM keyboard events or intentionally discard its suffix. However, the AppKit/NSTextInputContext interpretation step determines whether that full native string becomes one insertion command, composition activity, a partial commit plus layout suffix, or some other command sequence.

For the reported `hoanx → hoaã` or transient `ê` rollback, the most plausible boundary is therefore **the synthesized CGEvent/AppKit text-input interpretation and event sequencing**, especially mixing repeated synthetic Backspaces with one key event carrying a multi-character Unicode payload—not an intentional WebKit “first UTF-16 character only” rule.

